  "text318": "CREW PROFILE",
  "text319": "Real science. Playful training.",
  "text320": [
    "<p>Astro Kitty turns engineering ideas into short, hands-on missions. This is an independent educational prototype for your Space Apps project.</p><p class=\"science-note\">All scores, timers, power units, growth rates and the exposure index are simplified game rules. These practice drills use simplified rules. The linked outpost game uses live NASA DONKI solar-flare records with a bundled archive fallback. It is not a spacecraft or astronaut safety simulator.</p><h3>01 · Dust & solar energy</h3><p>NASA documents how dust reduced InSight’s solar output over years. The fast cleaning mission is our own playable interpretation.</p><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/insights-power-generation-after-landing-and-spring-2022/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA · InSight’s power generation ↗</a><h3>02 · Surface logistics</h3><p>Supply collection practices route planning. The rover, crates, fuel and rock penalties are fictional; they do not use rover engineering datasets.</p><h3>03 · A habitat’s competing needs</h3><p>Real life support includes water recovery, oxygen generation and carbon-dioxide removal. The game reserves a fixed essential load; a crop tray provides supplemental food. Our three shifts and growing times are accelerated.</p><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/reference/environmental-control-and-life-support-systems-eclss/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA · Environmental control & life support ↗</a><h3>04 · Solar-particle alerts</h3><p>Curiosity recorded a large solar storm at Mars in May 2024. Our shelter mission is inspired by that event; it uses a fictional countdown and exposure index, with no conversion to health effects.</p><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/mars/nasa-watches-mars-light-up-during-epic-solar-storm/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA · Mars during the 2024 solar storm ↗</a><h3>About this prototype</h3><p>Created for Astro Kitty’s Junior Astronaut Trainer concept. Original vector artwork and a lightweight HTML, CSS and JavaScript game loop. The game works without API keys or a game account. NASA is a science source; no endorsement is implied.</p>"
  ],
  "text321": "FIELD NOTES / SCIENCE LOG",
  "text322": "A little flight guidance",
  "text323": [
    "<p>Choose a mission card, read its goals, then press <strong>Start mission</strong>. All six missions are open so you can explore in any order.</p><div class=\"help-grid\"><div><strong>Walk & drive</strong><p>Use arrow keys or WASD. On touchscreens, hold the direction buttons or tap a destination.</p></div><div><strong>Solar sweep</strong><p>Hold Space near a panel. Or tap a panel and Kitty will walk over and clean automatically.</p></div><div><strong>Power planning</strong><p>Adjust the three sliders. Check the predicted outcome and total budget before running each shift.</p></div><div><strong>Take a break</strong><p>Press P or the pause icon. Your timer stops. You can retry every mission and keep your best result.</p></div></div><p>Earn up to 1,000 XP and three stars per mission. Better time, careful driving and efficient planning improve your score. Every completed mission earns at least one star.</p><p class=\"saved-note\">Sound is optional and off by default. Progress is saved on this browser, not shared between devices.</p>"
  ],
